  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nH5 troughing is taking shape across the Pacific Northwest and \nNorthern US Rockies today, which will gradually deepen Tuesday. An \nupper low forms Tuesday evening, moving northeastward out of Wyoming \nand into the Northern Plains by early Wednesday morning. This will \nbe our next source of precipitation for parts of the area. Ensemble \nguidance has shifted slightly northward in the track, which moves \nmost of the heavier precip potential into north central North Dakota \nand southwestern Manitoba. Locations generally north and west of \nDevils Lake could see up a couple hundredths of rain Wednesday \nnight through Thursday mid morning. Probability for greater than\n0.10 inch is less than 25 percent. \n\n...Rain and Snow Friday into Saturday...\n\nAnother H5 low is present within most ensemble guidance as we head \ninto Friday and Saturday. As with the previous system, the track has \nshifted slightly since the last forecast update; however, the \npotential scenarios only show minimal changes. Precipitation will \nlikely start as rain, with a rain/snow line emerging late Friday \nnight, then slowly transitioning to snow from west to east Saturday \nmorning. The best chances for accumulating snow will be in northeast \nNorth Dakota, where temperatures will be around or just below \nfreezing. At this time, the probability for 2+ inches of snow \nremains around 40 percent. The probability of 2+ inches falls to \naround 20 percent in the central Red River Valley. Further south and \neast, any potential snowfall is likely to encounter warmer \ntemperatures and melting, at least initially. Winds are expected to \nincrease as the low moves off to the northeast Saturday afternoon. \nNorthwest winds could gust upwards of 35 mph.",
